/*  FUNCTION NAME:      GetVar
    DESCRIPTION:*//**Reads a named variable from a state string. Returns a native-typed scalar or vertical array based on the entry's type tag. Broadcasts over a column of states (scalar variables only).*/
/*  RETURNS:            value: the named variable's value from the state (scalar or vertical array, native type)
    PARAMETERS:
        state           (Required) Single state string, or a vertical column of states (e.g. a SCAN result).
        name            (Required) Variable name to read.
    EXAMPLE:            =GetVar(SetVar(NewState(), "x", 5), "x") -> 5 (number — element type recovered from the tag)
    NOTE:               Tag values: n=number, b=boolean, t=text. When state is a column, the variable must be scalar — array values collapse to their first element under broadcast.
    REVISIONS:          Date        Developer   Description
                        2026-04-27  Claude      Initial version
                        2026-04-29  Claude      Read per-entry tag and coerce to native type
                        2026-04-29  Claude      Broadcast over a column of states via MAP
                        2026-11-01  Claude      Deref a single state with @: a 1x1-array state (e.g. INDEX of a SCAN inside a LAMBDA) made TEXTSPLIT keep only its first entry (#376)
                        2026-11-06  Claude      Find the entry with TEXTAFTER/TEXTBEFORE instead of splitting the whole state (spec 0011 part 6)
                        2026-11-09  Claude      Help table removed from the formula; its text is the header metadata the argument hints read (#381, #382)
*/
GetVar = LAMBDA(
//  Parameter Declarations
    [state],
    [name],
    LET(
    //  Procedure
        us, CHAR(31),
        rs, CHAR(30),
        gs, CHAR(29),
        key, us & @name & rs,
        single?, AND(ROWS(state) = 1, COLUMNS(state) = 1),
    //  Inline, not a helper LAMBDA: building one costs ~20% of a hot-loop step (spec 0011 appendix)
        result, IF(single?,
                LET(
                //  Match mode 1 ignores case, like =. The key on the end means TEXTAFTER always finds
                //  it (a state shorter than the key gives #VALUE!, even with if_not_found), so a
                //  missing name reads as an empty entry.
                    entry, TEXTBEFORE(TEXTAFTER(us & @state & key, key, 1, 1) & us, us),
                    tag, LEFT(entry, 1),
                    payload, MID(entry, 3, LEN(entry)),
                    raw, IF(ISNUMBER(FIND(gs, payload)), TOCOL(TEXTSPLIT(payload, gs)), payload),
                    IF(tag = "n", --raw, IF(tag = "b", raw = "TRUE", IF(tag = "", NA(), raw)))
                ),
                MAP(state, LAMBDA(s, GetVar(s, name)))
            ),
        result
)
);
